            "output": "<path>/Users/savekirk/dev/ai/entire/external-agents/.claude/skills/entire-external-agent/research.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: name: research\n3: description: >\n4:   Phase 1: Assess a target agent's capabilities and produce a protocol mapping\n5:   for building an external agent binary. Use /entire-external-agent research\n6:   or /entire-external-agent:research when you only need the research phase.\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # Research Procedure\n10: \n11: Assess a target agent's capabilities and produce a protocol mapping for building an external agent binary.\n12: \n13: ## Prerequisites\n14: \n15: Ensure the following parameters are available (from the orchestrator or user):\n16: - `AGENT_NAME` — Human-readable agent name\n17: - `AGENT_SLUG` — Kebab-case slug for the binary name\n18: - `PROJECT_DIR` — Where the project will be created\n19: \n20: ## Phase 1: Understand the External Agent Protocol\n21: \n22: Read the protocol specification to understand what subcommands and response formats the binary must implement.\n23: \n24: **Protocol spec:**\n25: 1. Read `https://github.com/entireio/cli/blob/main/docs/architecture/external-agent-protocol.md` — full protocol spec (subcommands, JSON schemas, capabilities)\n26: 2. Read `https://github.com/entireio/cli/blob/main/cmd/entire/cli/agent/external/types.go` — JSON response type definitions\n27: 3. Read `https://github.com/entireio/cli/blob/main/cmd/entire/cli/agent/external/external.go` — how the CLI invokes each subcommand (args, stdin, expected stdout)\n28: \n29: If the user provides a different protocol spec location explicitly, read that instead.\n30: \n31: Key things to note:\n32: - Which subcommands are always required vs. capability-gated\n33: - The JSON schema for each response type\n34: - The HookInput, AgentSession, and Event object formats\n35: - Environment variables set on every invocation (`ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT`, `ENTIRE_PROTOCOL_VERSION`)\n36: \n37: ## Phase 2: Investigate the Target Agent\n38: \n39: Ask the user: \"Do you have documentation or specs for the target agent's hook/lifecycle system? Or should I auto-research?\"\n40: \n41: ### If user provides docs:\n42: Read the provided docs and extract:\n43: - Hook/lifecycle mechanism (how to register callbacks)\n44: - Session management (where sessions are stored, how IDs work)\n45: - Transcript format and location\n46: - Configuration file format and location\n47: \n48: ### If auto-research:\n49: \n50: #### Step 1: Binary probing\n51: \n52: Run non-destructive CLI checks. Record PASS/WARN/FAIL for each:\n53: \n54: | Check | Command | PASS | FAIL |\n55: |-------|---------|------|------|\n56: | Binary present | `command -v <agent-binary>` | Found | Not found |\n57: | Help output | `<agent-binary> --help` or `<agent-binary> help` | Available | No help |\n58: | Version info | `<agent-binary> --version` | Available | N/A |\n59: | Hook keywords | Scan help for: hook, lifecycle, callback, event, trigger, pre-, post-, plugin, extension | Found | None found |\n60: | Session keywords | Scan help for: session, resume, continue, history, transcript, context | Found | None found |\n61: | Config directory | Check `~/.<agent-slug>/`, `~/.config/<agent-slug>/`, `./<agent-slug>/`, `./.${agent-slug}/` | Found | None found |\n62: \n63: #### Step 2: Documentation search\n64: \n65: Use web search to find:\n66: - The agent's official hook/plugin/extension documentation\n67: - How to register lifecycle callbacks\n68: - Session/transcript storage format and location\n69: - Configuration file format\n70: \n71: #### Step 3: Config and session directory exploration\n72: \n73: If a config directory was found:\n74: 1. List its contents (non-destructively)\n75: 2. Look for settings files (JSON, YAML, TOML)\n76: 3. Look for session/history directories\n77: 4. Look for transcript files and determine their format\n78: \n79: #### Step 4: Map agent concepts to protocol subcommands\n80: \n81: For each protocol subcommand, determine:\n82: - Whether the agent has a native concept that maps to it\n83: - How to implement it (which native API/config/file to use)\n84: - Whether it's straightforward or requires workarounds\n85: \n86: Create a mapping table:\n87: \n88: | Subcommand | Native Concept | Implementation Notes | Feasibility |\n89: |-----------|---------------|---------------------|-------------|\n90: | `info` | — | Static metadata, always implementable | Required |\n91: | `detect` | — | Check for binary or config | Required |\n92: | `get-session-id` | (agent's session ID mechanism) | ... | Required |\n93: | `get-session-dir` | (agent's session directory) | ... | Required |\n94: | `resolve-session-file` | (how agent stores sessions) | ... | Required |\n95: | `read-session` | (session data format) | ... | Required |\n96: | `write-session` | (session data persistence) | ... | Required |\n97: | `read-transcript` | (transcript file location) | ... | Required |\n98: | `chunk-transcript` | (raw bytes, language-generic) | Base64 chunking | Required |\n99: | `reassemble-transcript` | (reverse of chunk) | Base64 reassembly | Required |\n100: | `format-resume-command` | (agent's resume mechanism) | ... | Required |\n101: | `parse-hook` | (agent's native hook events) | ... | If hooks capable |\n102: | `install-hooks` | (agent's hook config format) | ... | If hooks capable |\n103: | `uninstall-hooks` | (reverse of install) | ... | If hooks capable |\n104: | `are-hooks-installed` | (check hook config) | ... | If hooks capable |\n105: | `get-transcript-position` | (file size/position) | ... | If transcript analyzer |\n106: | `extract-modified-files` | (parse transcript for file ops) | ... | If transcript analyzer |\n107: | `extract-prompts` | (parse transcript for user msgs) | ... | If transcript analyzer |\n108: | `extract-summary` | (parse transcript for summaries) | ... | If transcript analyzer |\n109: \n110: #### Step 5: Select capabilities\n111: \n112: Based on the mapping, determine which capabilities the binary should declare:\n113: - `hooks` — Can the agent be configured to call external commands on lifecycle events?\n114: - `transcript_analyzer` — Does the agent produce parseable transcripts?\n115: - `transcript_preparer` — Does the transcript need pre-processing?\n116: - `token_calculator` — Does the transcript contain token usage data?\n117: - `text_generator` — Can the agent's LLM be invoked for text generation?\n118: - `hook_response_writer` — Does the agent support writing messages back via hooks?\n119: - `subagent_aware_extractor` — Does the agent spawn subagents with their own transcripts?\n120: \n121: ## Phase 3: Verification Script\n122: \n123: Docs tend to be out of date. Before writing the one-pager, create a verification script that captures real payloads from the target agent to confirm Phase 2 findings.\n124: \n125: Based on Phase 2 findings, create an **agent-specific** test script:\n126: \n127: ```\n128: <PROJECT_DIR>/scripts/verify-<AGENT_SLUG>.sh\n129: ```\n130: \n131: The script is tailored to the specific agent's hook mechanism (not a generic template). Adapt the hook wiring section based on what Phase 2 discovered.\n132: \n133: **Script structure:**\n134: \n135: ```bash\n136: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n137: set -euo pipefail\n138: \n139: AGENT_NAME=\"...\"\n140: AGENT_SLUG=\"...\"\n141: AGENT_BIN=\"...\"\n142: PROBE_DIR=\"<PROJECT_DIR>/.probe-${AGENT_SLUG}-$(date +%s)\"\n143: ```\n144: \n145: **Required sections:**\n146: \n147: 1. **Static checks** — Re-runnable binary/version/help checks from Phase 2\n148: 2. **Hook wiring** — Create workspace-local config that intercepts the agent's hooks and dumps stdin JSON to `$PROBE_DIR/captures/<event-name>-<timestamp>.json`. Use the agent's native hook config format discovered in Phase 2.\n149: 3. **Run modes:**\n150:    - `--run-cmd '<cmd>'` — Automated: launch agent with a non-interactive prompt, wait, collect captures\n151:    - `--manual-live` — Interactive: user runs agent manually