es a multi-turn session)\n179: \n180: These give you specific strings to search for across all storage locations.\n181: \n182: #### Step 2: Inspect session/transcript files for actual content\n183: \n184: Read the session files identified in Phase 2 and check each assistant message:\n185: - Does it contain the **actual response text** you saw in the UI, or just a placeholder like `\"On it.\"`, `\"Working...\"`, `\"Sure\"`, or empty string?\n186: - Are **tool calls** recorded with their names, inputs, and outputs?\n187: - Is the `completion` / `response` / `content` field populated or empty?\n188: - Do `promptLogs` or similar fields contain the full model output?\n189: \n190: **If assistant content is placeholder-only, the agent stores real data elsewhere.** Proceed to Step 3.\n191: \n192: #### Step 3: Find ALL recently modified files\n193: \n194: Before running a prompt, create a timestamp marker. After the prompt completes, find everything the IDE touched:\n195: \n196: ```bash\n197: touch /tmp/before-marker\n198: # ... run your prompt in the IDE ...\n199: find ~/Library/Application\\ Support/<agent>/ -type f -newer /tmp/before-marker 2>/dev/null\n200: # Linux: find ~/.config/<agent>/ -type f -newer /tmp/before-marker\n201: ```\n202: \n203: This reveals hidden storage that documentation may not mention — execution logs, per-turn action traces, content-addressed caches, etc.\n204: \n205: #### Step 4: Search for your distinctive response\n206: \n207: Grep the entire IDE data directory for the known response text from your test prompts:\n208: \n209: ```bash\n210: grep -rl \"your distinctive response text\" ~/Library/Application\\ Support/<agent>/ 2>/dev/null\n211: ```\n212: \n213: This definitively locates where the real data lives. Read the matching files to understand their format.\n214: \n215: #### Step 5: Map the IDE's internal ID system\n216: \n217: Look for cross-referencing IDs that link session files to the real data:\n218: - Session IDs, execution IDs, conversation IDs, workspace IDs\n219: - How they cross-reference (e.g., session file has `executionId` → execution log has matching field)\n220: - Whether IDs are deterministic (computable from workspace path or session ID) or opaque (require scanning)\n221: - Whether directory names are hashes, base64-encoded paths, or opaque\n222: \n223: #### Step 6: Verify hook data flow\n224: \n225: Confirm hooks actually receive the data you expect:\n226: - Create a diagnostic hook that dumps everything to a file: `sh -c 'cat > /tmp/hook-dump-$(date +%s).json'`\n227: - For each hook type, check: Does stdin contain JSON? Are env vars set? Are args passed?\n228: - **Watch for stdin redirects** — some IDEs wrap hook commands in `sh -c '... </dev/null'` which blocks stdin data flow\n229: - If hooks receive empty input, determine if the IDE passes data via environment variables, command arguments, or not at all\n230: \n231: #### Step 7: Document findings\n232: \n233: Record what you found for the one-pager:\n234: - Primary storage: where session/conversation state lives (even if incomplete)\n235: - Secondary storage: where the real response/tool data lives (execution logs, databases, etc.)\n236: - Cross-reference mechanism: how to link primary → secondary (IDs, timestamps, etc.)\n237: - Hook data availability: what each hook type actually receives\n238: \n239: ## Phase 4: Write the One-Pager\n240: \n241: Create `<PROJECT_DIR>/AGENT.md` (create the directory first if needed).\n242: \n243: **Important:** Use ground-truth values from verification script captures (Phase 3a) wherever available. If a field was verified by real payloads, mark it as `(verified)`. If based only on docs/probing, mark it as `(unverified)`.\n244: \n245: Template:\n246: \n247: ```markdown\n248: # <AGENT_NAME> — External Agent Research\n249: \n250: ## Verdict: COMPATIBLE / PARTIAL / INCOMPATIBLE\n251: \n252: ## Static Checks\n253: | Check | Result | Notes |\n254: |-------|--------|-------|\n255: | Binary present | PASS/FAIL | path |\n256: | Help available | PASS/FAIL | |\n257: | Version info | PASS/FAIL | version string |\n258: | Hook keywords | PASS/FAIL | keywords found |\n259: | Session keywords | PASS/FAIL | keywords found |\n260: | Config directory | PASS/FAIL | path |\n261: | Documentation | PASS/FAIL | URL |\n262: \n263: ## Binary\n264: - Name: `<agent-binary>`\n265: - Version: ...\n266: - Install: ... (how to install if not present)\n267: \n268: ## Hook Mechanism\n269: - Config file: (exact path)\n270: - Config format: JSON / YAML / TOML\n271: - Hook registration: (how hooks are declared)\n272: - Hook names and protocol mapping:\n273:   | Native Hook Name | When It Fires | Protocol Event Type |\n274:   |-----------------|---------------|---------------------|\n275:   | ... | ... | 1=SessionStart, 2=TurnStart, etc. |\n276: - Hook input format: (what data is passed to hooks — stdin, env vars, args)\n277: \n278: ## Session Management\n279: - Session directory: (where sessions are stored)\n280: - Session ID source: (how to extract from hook input or filesystem)\n281: - Session file format: (JSON, JSONL, binary, etc.)\n282: \n283: ## Transcript\n284: - Location: (path pattern)\n285: - Format: JSONL / JSON array / other\n286: - User prompt field: (which JSON field contains user prompts)\n287: - Modified files field: (which JSON field contains file operations)\n288: - Token usage field: (if available)\n289: - Example entry: `{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"...\"}`\n290: \n291: ## Data Storage Verification\n292: - Session files contain actual assistant content: YES / NO — PLACEHOLDER ONLY\n293: - If placeholder: what do session files contain instead? (e.g., \"On it.\", empty completion field)\n294: - Secondary storage location: (path pattern for execution logs, databases, etc. — or \"none found\")\n295: - Secondary storage format: (JSON per execution, SQLite, JSONL, etc.)\n296: - Cross-reference key: (how session entries link to secondary storage, e.g., executionId field)\n297